/**

@page exahype_performance_optimisation Performance optimisation


This section discusses some ExaHyPE-specific performance optimisation steps.
While ExaHyPE and its baseline, Peano, provide simplistic performance analysis 
features to spot some flaws, a proper performance optimisation would likely
rely on external performance analysis tools.
Please read @ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic performance optimisation remarks" parallel to this 
document.
All of the recommendations on  apply for ExaHyPE, too.



## Single core optimisation

### The code is extremely bandwidth-bound and seems to move data forth and back all the time

Most ExaHyPE solvers store their data on (call) stacks to localise all memory 
accesses. This implies that they move their data around a lot. This 
materialises in profiles similar to

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Top Hotspots
Function                                                           Module           CPU Time  % of CPU Time(%)
-----------------------------------------------------------------  --------------  ---------  ----------------
applications::exahype2::ccz4::ncp                                  peano4_bbhtest   559.299s             13.3%
__memmove_avx_unaligned_erms                                       libc.so.6        529.383s             12.6%
toolbox::blockstructured::interpolateHaloLayer_AoS_tensor_product  peano4_bbhtest   505.116s             12.0%
applications::exahype2::ccz4::source                               peano4_bbhtest   347.667s              8.3%
__kmpc_set_lock                                                    libiomp5.so      340.473s              8.1%
[Others]                                                           N/A             1916.907s             45.7%
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

where memory movements (__memmove_avx_unaligned_erms) end up high on the list.
You can alter 
this behaviour by switching to a heap-based memory administration. Consult the 
documentation of the solver of interest, but the ``fix'' always looks similar to 

         self._patch.generator = peano4.datamodel.PatchToDoubleArrayOnHeap(self._patch,"double")

i.e. you have to swap how a patch is mapped onto a C++ implementation. Most solvers provide a routine

         my_solver.switch_to_heap_storage(True, True)


which does the switch for you. Please around with different True, False 
combinations. 
Once you have switched, you should also consult @ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic remarks on alignment", 
and you might want to change your kernel into a variant which optimises aggressively for vector registers (and can 
potentially even use GPUs). This topic is discussed below.





### The PDE term routines (sources, ncp, fluxes, eigenvalues, ...) are extremely expensive 

In principle, that's what you want to see. If this is the case, then you should next follow the recommendations for GPU
parallelisation. These focus on GPU efficiency, but the optimisations also pay off significantly for CPUs.




## Multithreading optimisation

A quick first insight into the single node behaviour can be obtained through Peano's
@ref peano_performance_optimisation "scaling analysis scripts".
Consult the @ref peano_runtime_analysis "Peano runtime analysis" remarks as well.



### There is a lack of enclave tasks

@todo Something about the statistics: Are there enough enclave tasks

This phenomenon often goes hand in hand with a lack of enclave tasks: The problem is
simply too small to find enough tasks.
There are a few tasks, but as tasks can only run on one single core, the
code struggles to keep all cores busy.
Check if you can work with more regular grids. They usually help you
to generate more tasks. So avoid steep AMR changes.


### The code does not use all cores

Hereon, we assume that you have doublechecked that the code tries to use one
subtree (partition) per core and that these partitions are reasonably balanced.
Details are discussed in \ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic optimisation discussion".
Furthermore, we assume that you have already introduced enclave tasking in 
your solver, and you also have validated that enough enclave tasks are 
produced (see above). In this case, it might be reasonable to switch the 
compute kernel.


For the FD4 scheme and OpenMP, we provide for example compute kernels which theoretically can spread out over the
whole node. The kernel is slower than the default version on one core, but
it can use further cores and thus eventually becomes faster. Usually, you
have to follow a few steps to enable this alternative kernel:

- Switch to an enclave solver.
- Instruct the enclave solver that there it can use stateless PDE
  terms. For this, pass the constructor the argument
  
        pde_terms_without_state=True
        
        
- Ensure that the routine patchCanUseStatelessPDETerms() return true most of the time (this is the default).
- Make the solver use a solver on the CPU which uses the batched taskgraph 
  realisation. Most ExaHyPE solver have an attribute similar to
  
  
        self._fused_compute_kernel_call_cpu
        
  which is initialised with some default kernel call. Browse through your 
  code and alter it such that it uses

        solver_variant                           = SolverVariant.Multicore,
        kernel_variant                           = KernelVariant.BatchedAoSHeap,

  and a batched solver variant (patch-wise or similar makes limited
  sense). Usually, the kernels are constructed in set_implementation(), so 
  it might make sense to plug into this one (overwrite it, call the 
  supertype's implementation, and then switch the kernel).


This idea corresponds to the last remark in the section 
"The individual trees are of significantly different sizes" of 
\ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic optimisation discussion".


### Using many enclave tasks makes my code slower rather than faster

This could either be a scheduler flaw, or you have too few enclave tasks.
The scheduler flaw is discussed in @ref peano_performance_optimisation "Peano's generic performance optimisation section", but
ExaHyPE offers a special command line argument to switch between different
tasking schemes. Run the code with help and use

     ---threading-model native
     
or another option afterwards to play around with various threading schemes.

If this does not work, switch to task-based kernels.

@todo Does the code use enclave tasks and yield many tasks? In this case,
    you might have encountered a scheduler flaw. Change the multicore scheduler
    on the command line.



